<quiz>
<question type="stack">
  <name><text>Median and quartiles of ungrouped data</text></name>
  <questiontext format="html">
    <text><![CDATA[<p>The masses, in kg, of \({@n@}\) bags of rice in a shop in Mombasa are listed below (not in order).</p><p style="text-align:center;">{@gstr@}</p><p>Give exact values.</p><p>(a) Find the median.</p><div>Median \(=\) [[input:ans1]] kg [[validation:ans1]] [[feedback:prt1]]</div><p>(b) Find the lower quartile, \(Q_1\).</p><div>\(Q_1 =\) [[input:ans2]] kg [[validation:ans2]] [[feedback:prt2]]</div><p>(c) Find the upper quartile, \(Q_3\).</p><div>\(Q_3 =\) [[input:ans3]] kg [[validation:ans3]] [[feedback:prt3]]</div>]]></text>
  </questiontext>
  <generalfeedback format="html">
    <text><![CDATA[<p>Put the data in order first. For a list of \(n\) values, the median is at position \(\dfrac{n+1}{2}\), \(Q_1\) at \(\dfrac{n+1}{4}\) and \(Q_3\) at \(\dfrac{3(n+1)}{4}\); \(Q_1\) has a quarter of the data at or below it. A position that is not a whole number lies between two values: go that fraction of the way from the lower value to the higher one.</p><p>In order: {@sstr@}.</p><p>(a) Position \(\dfrac{ {@n+1@} }{2} = {@exd(p2)@}\): halfway between \({@s[n/2]@}\) and \({@s[n/2+1]@}\), so the median is \({@exd(med)@}\) kg.</p><p>(b) Position \(\dfrac{ {@n+1@} }{4} = {@exd(p1)@}\): \({@s[floor(p1)]@} + {@exd(fp1)@} \times ({@s[floor(p1)+1]@} - {@s[floor(p1)]@}) = {@exd(q1)@}\) kg.</p><p>(c) Position \(\dfrac{3 \times {@n+1@} }{4} = {@exd(p3)@}\): \({@s[floor(p3)]@} + {@exd(fp3)@} \times ({@s[floor(p3)+1]@} - {@s[floor(p3)]@}) = {@exd(q3)@}\) kg.</p>]]></text>
  </generalfeedback>
  <defaultgrade>3</defaultgrade>
  <penalty>0.1</penalty>
  <hidden>0</hidden>
  <idnumber></idnumber>
  <stackversion><text>2026063000</text></stackversion>
  <questionvariables>
    <text><![CDATA[/* exd: shortest exact decimal display of a terminating value (up to 3 d.p.) */
exd(v) := if integerp(v) then v else if integerp(10*v) then dispdp(float(v),1) else if integerp(100*v) then dispdp(float(v),2) else dispdp(float(v),3);
/* Design: median and quartiles of a list using (n+1) positions. n is 14 or 18, so every position is fractional. Source: adapted from SMP AS/A2 Statistics 1, chapter 1, section B, B2. */
dl: [[51,46,48,53,43,29,36,50,16,17,41,22,19,15],[31,39,21,51,44,27,33,40,59,45,17,56,24,57,47,48,10,34],[57,44,42,18,40,13,12,37,39,34,22,24,35,46],[51,22,42,52,58,43,53,49,50,30,57,36,20,26,17,34,21,47],[50,46,25,31,55,41,53,57,42,34,13,24,51,29],[47,55,51,14,36,42,24,46,31,25,54,33,32,50,22,35,16,57],[38,36,49,11,43,46,47,53,40,35,25,52,59,31],[26,32,41,22,31,46,35,17,28,54,56,48,16,52,44,59,27,30],[25,54,31,39,14,43,37,50,57,53,15,30,21,12],[17,40,47,36,50,59,15,22,39,58,28,33,37,38,55,44,23,51]];
ix: 1+rand(10);
g: dl[ix];
n: length(g);
s: sort(g);
qv(L,p) := L[floor(p)] + (p-floor(p))*(L[floor(p)+1]-L[floor(p)]);
med: qv(s,(n+1)/2);
q1: qv(s,(n+1)/4);
q3: qv(s,3*(n+1)/4);
gstr: simplode(map(string,g),", ");
sstr: simplode(map(string,s),", ");
p1: (n+1)/4; p2: (n+1)/2; p3: 3*(n+1)/4;
fp1: p1-floor(p1); fp3: p3-floor(p3);
/* diagnostics */
medu: qv(g,(n+1)/2);
medn: s[n/2];
q1u: qv(g,(n+1)/4);
q1n: qv(s,n/4);
q1fl: s[floor((n+1)/4)];
q1h: s[(n/2+1)/2];
q3u: qv(g,3*(n+1)/4);
q3n: qv(s,3*n/4);
q3fl: s[floor(3*(n+1)/4)];]]></text>
  </questionvariables>
